# Security, Auth, and Environment

## Secrets

- Never expose secrets, API keys, private tokens, passwords, Sanity write tokens, service-role keys, or auth secrets in client-side code.
- Never hard-code secrets. Never print secret values in terminal output, logs, docs, commits, or reports.
- Keep `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.production`, and equivalent secret files gitignored.
- `.env.example` may list variable names and safe placeholders only — never real secret values.
- When documenting env vars, document names and purpose, never secret values.

## AuthN / AuthZ

- Authentication and CMS content management are separate concerns.
- Do not store user accounts as plaintext credentials in Sanity. Never use Sanity as a password database. Never store plaintext passwords.
- Use secure password hashing or a reputable authentication provider.
- Sessions must use secure, appropriately configured cookies or tokens.
- Enforce authentication and authorization **server-side**. The UI is not a security boundary.
- Never trust roles, permissions, user IDs, or ownership values supplied by the client.
- Owner / Admin / Editor / Employee permissions must be explicit and documented.

## Input and app security

- Validate and sanitize all external input.
- Protect against XSS, CSRF, injection, IDOR, privilege escalation, broken access control, open redirects, path traversal, unsafe uploads, brute-force attacks, and sensitive error leakage.
